<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="arsenic detoxification (plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; While the jury is still out about whether Napoleon Bonaparte died of arsenic poisoning or cancer |CITS: [367316][6750413]|, it is agreed by all that arsenic is a potent toxicant. Indeed, the results of exposure to high arsenic concentration in drinking water in West Bengal, India, and Bangladesh are considered by some to be the worst public health disaster in recent history |CITS: [15276412]|. The form of arsenic influences the type and severity of toxicity. Arsenate (As&lt;sup&gt;V&lt;/sup&gt;) is a structural analog of phosphate, and inhibits phosphorylation processes. For example, ADP-arsenate spontaneously hydrolyzes, resulting in uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation |CITS: [518594]|, while arsenite (As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt;) has a very high affinity for thiol groups, and thus binds to and inhibits many key enzymes that have thiol groups in their active sites. In addition, inorganic arsenic is a potent carcinogen |CITS: [12505313]|. Arsenic exists in two oxidation states, As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; and As&lt;sup&gt;V&lt;/sup&gt;. In aerobic environments most of the inorganic arsenic is found as As&lt;sup&gt;V&lt;/sup&gt;, and in neutral pH the common species is |FRAME: ARSENATE|. Most (but not all) of the inorganic As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; in neutral pH exists as the non-charged species |FRAME: CPD0-2040|. Both As&lt;sup&gt;V&lt;/sup&gt; and As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; compounds can enter the cell. |FRAME: ARSENATE Arsenate| is chemically similar to phosphate, and enters the cell via the phosphate transport systems, such as the |FRAME:TAX-562| |FRAME: PITA-MONOMER| and |FRAME: ABC-27-CPLX| systems. |FRAME: CPD0-2040 &quot;Arsenious acid&quot;| is a polyol similar to glycerol, and can enter cells via bidirectional channels that allow water and small uncharged molecules like glycerol to pass through (such as |FRAME: EG10396 GlpF| of |FRAME: TAX-562| and aquaglyceroporins). The absorption rate of |FRAME: ARSENATE| is much lower than that of |FRAME: CPD0-2040|, and the role of phosphate transporters in the uptake of arsenic appears to be limited. For example, bacterial periplasmic phosphate-binding proteins discriminate phosphate over |FRAME: CPD0-2040| more than 500- to 850-fold (up to 4500-fold in the extremely arsenic-resistance species |FRAME: TAX-1118153|) |CITS: [32435915]|. Both prokaryotes and eukaryotes reduce intracellular arsenate to arsenite. However, prokaryotes and single-cell eukaryotes can pump arsenite out of the cell by using a dedicated pump (such as the |FRAME: G3O-34321-MONOMER Acr3| or |FRAME: CPLX-7 ArsB|), multicellular organisms require other means of getting rid of the metal. Certain mammals, including humans, methylate arsenite to several methylated forms that are oxidized to non-toxic methylated As(V) forms and excreted from the organism in the urine. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Arsenic compounds can enter the plant cell by similar mechanisms to those observed in other organisms. Since |FRAME: ARSENATE| is chemically similar to phosphate, it enters most cells via phosphate transporters such as the |FRAME: MONOMER-21678|. |FRAME: CPD0-2040 &quot;Arsenous acid&quot;|, the neutral form of |FRAME: CPD-763|, can enter the cell via aquaglyceroproteins such as |FRAME: MONOMER-21673| in |FRAME: TAX-3702| or |FRAME: MONOMER-21679| in rice |CITS: [16572174][19029297]|. Once inside cells, |FRAME: ARSENATE| is reduced to the more txoic |FRAME: CPD-763| by |FRAME: EC-1.20.4.1|. There has been some controversy about the identity of the gene encoding the enzyme in plants. The |FRAME: TAX-3702| genome, as well as the genomes of several other plants, contain homologs of the yeast |FRAME: G3O-34320| gene (such as atACR2), and these genes were claimed to encode the plant arsenate reductase |CITS: [16507083][16766666][17388894]|. There were several indications that the gene functions as an arsenate reductase - for example, it was able to complement an |FRAME: TAX-562| arsenate reductase mutant, and an AtACR2 knockout line showed no arsenate reductase activity |CITS: [15834011]| and displayed a strong As(V)-sensitive phenotype |CITS: [16567632]|. However, in 2012 it was shown that knocking out ACR2 does not affect arsenic redox status in |FRAME: TAX-3702|, and it was suggested that the gene does not encode a functional arsenate reductase |CITS: [22879969]|. A different gene, |FRAME: AT2G21045|, was discovered in 2014, and appeared to encode an active enzyme |CITS: [25464340][25099865][28695678]|. When expressed in an |FRAME: TAX-562| |FRAME: EG12237| mutant, the gene complemented the mutation, resulting in a similar As(V) resistance to the wild type strain |CITS: [25099865]|. Unlike mammals, plants do not have an |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.137|, and arsenite must be exported. In contrast to yeast, mammals, and many microorganisms in which arsenic species form complexes with glutatione, in plants detoxification of arsenic has been shown to mainly depend on |FRAME: Poly-Gamma-Glutamylcysteine-Glycines phytochelatins| (PCs) |CITS: [10368185][16313638][20192735][20130102]| (see |FRAME: PWY-6745|). Vacuolar transporters such as |FRAME: AT1G30400-MONOMER| (|FRAME: AT1G30400|) have been shown to export the arsenite-phytochelatin complexes into the vacuoles, where presumably the complex is broken down, leaving the arsenic inside the vacuole while the phytochelatin is exported back into the cytoplasm. An additional route for arsenite export has been reported in some plants such as rice that have dedicated silicon transporters. The |FRAME: G-107426| gene encodes an active efflux transporter, driven by the proton gradient across the plasma membrane. While the transporter&apos;s physiological role is to transport silicon out of root cells so that it could be translocated to the xylem, it is also transporting arsenite outside the cell |CITS: [17625566][18626020]|.</Description>
