    <DataNode elementId="ARSENATE" textLabel="arsenate" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="DJHGAFSJWGLOIV-UHFFFAOYSA-L" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="500" centerY="540" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment>Arsenic is the most prevalent environmental toxic substance. It ranks first on the priority list of Hazardous Substances published by the Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry (ATSDR), based on a combination of frequency, toxicity, and potential for human exposure. It is also a Group 1 human carcinogen associated with skin, bladder and lung cancer. While arsenic exists in two oxidation states, As(III) and As(V), in aerobic environments most inorganic arsenic is found as As(V), such as arsenic acid. The prevalent form of arsenic acid under neutral pH is |FRAME: ARSENATE arsenate&lt;sup&gt;2-&lt;/sup&gt;|. At neutral pH values arsenate exists mostly in the |FRAME: ARSENATE &quot;AsO&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;&lt;sup&gt;2-&lt;/sup&gt;&quot;| form (pKa&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt;= 2:3; pKa&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; = 6:8; and pKa&lt;sup&gt;3&lt;/sup&gt; = 11:6) |CITS: [11161507]|. |FRAME: ARSENATE Arsenate| is chemically similar to phosphate, and enters most cells via phosphate transporters. Once inside cells, arsenate is removed by assorted detoxification mechanisms, described in pathways under |FRAME: Arsenate-Detoxification|.</Comment>
