    <DataNode elementId="CPD0-2040" textLabel="arsenous acid" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="GCPXMJHSNVMWNM-UHFFFAOYSA-N" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="650" centerY="540" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="AU4FS-4, SRI, kothari">Arsenic is the most prevalent environmental toxic substance. It ranks first on the priority list of Hazardous Substances published by the Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry (ATSDR), based on a combination of frequency, toxicity, and potential for human exposure. It is also a Group 1 human carcinogen associated with skin, bladder and lung cancer. While arsenic exists in two oxidation states, As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; and As&lt;sup&gt;V&lt;/sup&gt;, in aerobic environments most inorganic arsenic is found as As(V), such as |FRAME: ARSENATE|. In anaerobic environments the prevalent form is As(III), such as |FRAME: CPD0-2040|. While most of the literature refers to the As(III) form found in the environment as |FRAME: CPD-763|, at natural pH values most of the As&lt;sup&gt;III&lt;/sup&gt; molecules exist in solution as |FRAME: CPD0-2040|, an inorganic polyol similar to glycerol, since the pKa values are high: pKa&lt;sup&gt;1&lt;/sup&gt; = 9:2 and pKa&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt; = 12:7 |CITS: [11161507][15106984]|. This form of arsenite can enter cells via glycerol channels such as |FRAME: EG10396 GlpF| of |FRAME: TAX-562| or aquaglyceroporins, bidirectional channels that allow water and small uncharged molecules such as glycerol to pass through. Charged forms of arsenite, formed at higher pH, include |FRAME: CPD-763 &quot;arsenite&lt;sup&gt;1-&lt;/sup&gt;&quot;|, |FRAME: CPD-24319| and |FRAME: CPD-24318|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPD0-2040" />
